---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SafetyNotice from '../components/SafetyNotice.astro';
import { pageTitle, breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={pageTitle('Safety')}
  description="Practical safety guidance for students exploring exam-prep and certification study communities."
  canonicalPath="/safety/"
  jsonLd={breadcrumbJsonLd([{ name: 'Safety', path: '/safety/' }])}
>
  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 sm:text-3xl">Safety</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-stone-600">
      A short, practical guide to staying safe while exploring study communities. Study-prep
      groups are overwhelmingly helpful — but they also attract scams aimed at stressed
      students. Here is what to watch for.
    </p>

    <SafetyNotice class="mt-6" />

    <div class="prose-static mt-8">
      <h2>Exam leaks and fake exam dumps</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Real exam content is protected.</strong> Anyone offering “today's real
          questions”, “verified leaks” or “braindumps with guaranteed questions” is almost
          certainly selling stolen content, invented content, or both.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Using leaks or dumps can get your scores cancelled.</strong> Exam bodies
          (College Board, ETS, Pearson VUE, CFA Institute, and others) investigate leaked
          material and routinely void scores, ban candidates, and pursue legal action.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>“Free dumps” are often bait.</strong> They are used to collect your email,
          install malware, or lure you into a paid scam. If a file is that good, it is not
          free.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Credential scams</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Nobody can pass for you.</strong> Offers to take your exam remotely, sit
          it for you, or “fix” your score are fraud. Exam centers verify identity, and
          impersonation can result in cancelled results and permanent bans.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Guaranteed pass guarantees are lies.</strong> No legitimate prep service
          can promise a specific score. “Pay after you pass” schemes still take your money
          and your personal data.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fake certificates are worthless and risky.</strong> Only the issuing body
          can award a credential. Buying a “certificate” without the exam is fraud — it
          will not survive an employer's verification check and can have legal
          consequences.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Fake tutors and impersonation</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Verify tutors independently.</strong> Anyone can copy a username, avatar
          or profile. Confirm a tutor's identity through the platform's official channels
          before paying or sharing anything.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Official-sounding names are not official.</strong> Accounts named after
          exam boards, universities or certification bodies are often impersonators.
          Exam boards never message students privately to demand payment or “re-register”
          you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Admins are not referees.</strong> A group admin or moderator is just
          another user with extra buttons. Treat private offers from admins the same way
          you would treat offers from strangers.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Payment scams</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Never pay to join or “unlock”.</strong> Legitimate study communities are
          free to join. Fees for invites, “vip access”, “verified materials” or “priority
          exam slots” are almost always scams.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Only pay exam bodies and known prep providers directly.</strong> Exam
          registration is never handled through a chat group. If someone in a group offers
          to “register” you, they are taking your money and your personal details.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No gift cards, no crypto, no “processing fees”.</strong> Requests for
          gift-card codes, cryptocurrency, or payments to random accounts are a
          near-certain scam, regardless of the story attached.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Phishing and malware in study files</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Check the URL before you click.</strong> Lookalike domains (e.g.
          <code>collegeboard-login.xyz</code>) are used to steal credentials. Log in to
          exam portals only by typing the official address yourself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Be careful with shared study files.</strong> PDFs and images are usually
          fine; executables, scripts, password-protected archives and “cracked” software
          are how malware spreads. Scan anything you download.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Never share codes or OTPs.</strong> One-time passwords, 2FA codes and
          verification codes are for you alone — no tutor, admin or “support agent” ever
          legitimately needs them.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Stolen course materials</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Pirated courses are both illegal and dangerous.</strong> Sharing or
          buying cracked prep courses violates copyright, and the files are frequently
          bundled with malware.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Recommend, don't redistribute.</strong> Pointing someone to an official
          course or free resource helps them. Sending them a stolen copy puts both of you
          at risk.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>General internet safety</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep personal details out of public groups.</strong> No full names,
          addresses, passport/ID numbers, or financial information in chats.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Turn on two-factor authentication</strong> on your email and messaging
          accounts — they are the keys to everything else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Trust the official channel.</strong> For anything exam-related —
          registration, scores, results, certification status — use the exam body's
          official website, never a chat link someone sent you.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What we check — and what we don't</h2>
      <p>
        We check whether invite links appear to work and record honest verification states
        (<a href="/how-we-verify/">see How We Verify</a>). We do <strong>not</strong> audit
        community content, members, admins, tutors or files shared inside a community.
        A checked link is a working link — nothing more.
      </p>

      <h2>Report problems</h2>
      <p>
        See a dead invite, suspected exam-dump trading, credential fraud, or malware being
        shared? Use the <a href="/report/">report form</a> — every report is reviewed by
        a human.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
